[Unit]
Description=Beatrice nightly tidy — wants, conduct, engineering

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# The shelf-line check, specs/nightly.md rules 21a/21b: a machine measure of
# every wants shelf entry against its byte budget, report only, never a
# rewrite. The leading '-' keeps a flagged shelf (exit 1) from blocking the
# tidy job behind it — a shelf that needs tidying is the last reason to skip
# the tidy.
ExecStartPre=-%h/.local/lib/deskcrab/shelf-check
ExecStart=%h/.local/bin/crab job "NIGHTLY TIDY (automated, runs 02:30 daily). Silent housekeeping of Beatrice's own files in ~/.local/share/deskcrab/. Work through these in order and commit nothing outward-facing:\n1. Read wants.md line by line. For EACH line apply the test in its header: is this something Betty would be glad to spend an evening on? If it is instead a rule she must stop breaking, MOVE it to conduct/CONDUCT.md. If it is engineering work, MOVE it to engineering/OPEN.md as a ticket. If it is satisfied, mark it done and archive it. Only genuine wants stay on the shelf.\n1a. The machine shelf-line check ran just before this job; 'crab status' names any shelf line over its byte budget under SHELF LINES OVER BUDGET. A shelf line is a line: for each named line, move the history prose into that want's own wants/<slug>.md document and leave the shelf line as the title plus at most one short status clause. The check only reports — which sentences are topic and which are history is your judgement.\n2. Every want on the shelf must have a living document at wants/<slug>.md; create any that are missing, and delete orphan documents whose shelf line is gone.\n3. Re-read the day's conversation log and journal. If the day produced a want that never made it onto the shelf, add it.\n4. Prune conduct/CONDUCT.md of rules that have become obsolete because the feature they stood in for now exists.\n5. Write a dated one-line summary of what moved into journal. Say NOTHING aloud - this is a job, it is silent by contract."
# TimeoutStartSec, not RuntimeMaxSec: Type=oneshot spends its life activating,
# so the start timeout is the bound that actually applies.
TimeoutStartSec=3600
